  "worked_solution": "When the circle intercepts the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$\n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-4)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 16+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y+4)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y+4)^{2}=-7.$ Therefore, the circle does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/2442ca38770f2f82e2e9c536632e8474adb75e04504a4273987989bb3d81b282.png)",
